<section class="bluf">
  <h2 class="tinylabel"><b>01</b> BLUF</h2>
  <h2>The Bottom Line:</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rebranded a newly merged public company end to end in one quarter, from identity through web, print, video, trade show, and product UI.</li>
    <li>Consolidated seven retired marks from the I.D. Systems and Pointer acquisitions into one brand with three product lines.</li>
    <li>Relaunched on NASDAQ as PWFL on October 3, 2019. The system ran every marketing and product surface until after my departure in 2022.</li>
    <li>Wrote the guidelines that let international subsidiaries apply the brand to their own materials and software without me.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="team">
  <h2 class="tinylabel"><b>02</b> My Role</h2>
  <p>Brand strategy, identity, brand guidelines, web, print and catalog, product and spec sheets, video (concept, edit, motion graphics, VO direction, audio post, 3D), trade show, product UI.</p>
</section>

<section class="long-form">
  <h2 class="tinylabel"><b>03</b> The Details</h2>

  <h3>The Problem</h3>

  <p>I.D. Systems acquired Pointer Telocation in 2019, and the combined company needed a new name, a new mark, and a way to make two companies' worth of products look like one company by the day the deal closed. Between them the portfolio carried at least seven legacy marks (I.D. Systems, CarrierWeb, KeyTroller, VeriWise, Asset Intelligence, ReeferMate, Trailer Mate) plus Pointer and Cellocator, each with its own type, color, and collateral.</p>

  <figure class="page">
    <%= image_tag "work/powerfleet_rebrand/guide-p30-retired-marks.jpg", loading: :lazy, alt: "The seven retired legacy marks consolidated into the PowerFleet brand" %>
    <figcaption>
      <p>
        Seven marks to one.
      </p>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The Mark</h3>

  <p>The new mark brings the I.D. Systems circle and the Pointer gradient together in a new, deeper red. The circle also reads as a &ldquo;P&rdquo; extracted from it. Product lines (Logistics, Industrial, Vehicles) sit under it as italic lockups. Pointer and Cellocator kept their own marks with a &ldquo;by PowerFleet&rdquo; endorsement; those brands had equity in their markets that wasn't worth spending.</p>

  <figure class="page">
    <%= image_tag "work/powerfleet_rebrand/guide-p19-main-mark.jpg", loading: :lazy, alt: "Guidelines page showing the PowerFleet prime mark and wordmark lockup" %>
    <figcaption>
      <p>
        The mark's rationale, from the guidelines.
      </p>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>The System</h3>

  <p>The guidelines covered voice, color, type (DIN Pro, with Bahnschrift for Office and Reforma for Hebrew), the mark and lockups, photography, graphic elements, trademarks, email signatures, and LinkedIn. Two parts did most of the work.</p>

  <p>The photo builder: a repeatable Photoshop recipe (desaturate, levels, a 186C vector shape at multiply, then again at 15 to 50 percent overlay) so anyone in marketing could produce on-brand imagery from stock without asking the design department.</p>

  <p>The graphic elements: a set of data callout components in two styles that carried the brand from marketing into product UI, and became the seed of the platform work below.</p>

  <figure class="page">
    <%= image_tag "work/powerfleet_rebrand/guide-p33-photo-builder.jpg", loading: :lazy, alt: "Guidelines page showing the photo builder recipe" %>
    <figcaption>
